<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<RecordType xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        RecordType.Description is capped at 255 characters. The full rationale below is
        operationally important, so it was moved here rather than trimmed; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. Do not paste this back into
        <description>. NOTE ALSO: the comment must sit INSIDE the root element - a comment
        placed ABOVE <RecordType> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error.

        DESCRIPTION TRIMMED 2026-08-09 - IT WAS 266 CHARACTERS AND WOULD HAVE FAILED THE DEPLOY
        with "Value too long for field: Description maximum length is:255", taking the whole
        package with it including the Apex (a custom object whose record types all fail cascades
        into "Variable does not exist: RecordTypeId"). Nothing was deleted - the two facts cut
        from <description> are restated here: the four states come from Part 2 lines 28-41, and
        Party_Role__c / Counter_Signed_Date__c are exposed on THIS record type only. This is the
        THIRD breach of the 255 cap in this programme (both Tranche 2 record types, then this
        one); the fix is always the same - long rationale belongs in this comment, and the
        <description> is a short summary. MEASURE the character count, do not eyeball it.

        THE SELL-SIDE NDA. Parented by Disposition__c. Four states:
        Not Sent -> Sent -> Signed, plus Declined as a terminal off-ramp
        (Part 2 lines 28-41). Not Sent and Declined DID NOT EXIST on the field before
        2026-08-09; both are added by this tranche and neither is reachable from the
        acquisition record type.

        WHY A RECORD TYPE AT ALL (decision D7). NDA__c is already dual-parented - Opportunity__c
        for acquisitions, Disposition__c for dispositions - and the two sides need DIFFERENT
        state sets. One shared restricted picklist cannot express that: the acquisition path
        has no Not Sent and no decline off-ramp, and the disposition path has no Pending and no
        Received. Record types give per-type value sets and per-type Paths, so neither side
        sees the other's states.

        SENT AND SIGNED ARE SHARED WITH THE ACQUISITION TYPE. Unlike LOI__c.Stage__c, whose two
        sets are fully disjoint, THE NDA SETS OVERLAP - so Status__c ALONE DOES NOT IDENTIFY THE
        RECORD TYPE. Anything that must discriminate (a flexipage visibility rule, a report
        filter, a future Mark Declined action) has to test Disposition__c or RecordTypeId, never
        Status__c on its own.

        =====================================================================
        ⚠ AMENDED 2026-08-14 (Phase 3, observation 5). THE CONTRAST ABOVE IS NOW FALSE. THE RULE
        IT ILLUSTRATES IS UNCHANGED, CORRECT, AND NOW APPLIES MORE WIDELY THAN WHEN IT WAS
        WRITTEN.
        =====================================================================

        RETRACTED, verbatim: "Unlike LOI__c.Stage__c, whose two sets are fully disjoint".
        Phase 3 repointed the acquisition LOI approval hop onto 'Under Review', a value
        Disposition_LOI has carried since 3B. LOI__c.Stage__c's two sets now OVERLAP at
        'Under Review'. ⚠ Their TERMINALS remain distinct ('Signed' vs 'Executed'), which is why
        LOI terminal-state tests still work by naming values; the overlap is in the MIDDLE of the
        sequence, which is the harder case to notice.

        🔴 NOTHING ABOUT THIS RECORD TYPE OR ITS RULE CHANGES - AND THE RULE IS STRENGTHENED, NOT
        WEAKENED. NDA__c was the cautionary exception ("unlike LOI"); LOI__c has now joined it, so
        this file's discipline - discriminate on Disposition__c or RecordTypeId, never on the
        status value alone - is now the DEFAULT for the acquisition/disposition object pairs
        rather than a special case. LOI_Record_Page paid for that in this same phase: its
        Submit_for_Approval entry keys on a stage value that is now shared, and it carries an
        open, undecided residual as a result (runbook 1.6 / gate G7).
        ⚠ DO NOT USE "the LOI sets are disjoint" AS PRIOR ART ANYWHERE AFTER THIS DATE. That
        sentence appears in several files; the ones found by sweep on 2026-08-14 are corrected,
        but this comment is the durable statement of the fact.

        WARNING - RECORD-TYPE PICKLIST RESTRICTION IS UI-ONLY. It is NOT enforced by Apex DML,
        so restricting Pending/Received out here does not stop a direct write. The Apex-side
        allow-list in RecordStageAdvanceService must become record-type-aware in the same
        tranche; that is the parallel developer change.

        THIS IS THE OFF-MARKET PATH'S FIRST STAGE. Disposition__c's Off_Market record type runs
        Disposition Readiness -> NDA -> Disposition Offer -> LOI -> PSA -> Closing -> Completed,
        so the NDA stage is where a direct sale actually starts, and the release gate
        All_NDAs_Signed_Before_Progression blocks EVERY forward stage from NDA onward until
        every NDA on the disposition is Signed (decision D15/Q4).

        DOCUSIGN IS NOT LICENSED YET (decision D2). These states are manually driven and the
        executed file is attached by hand; Method__c already carries a DocuSign value, so an
        envelope-status callback can later drive Status__c without reshaping this picklist or
        the gate that reads it.

        EVERY PICKLIST ON THE OBJECT IS ENUMERATED - Status__c, Method__c and Party_Role__c -
        because a record type file that omits a picklist silently drops all of its values from
        that type. Precedent: objects/Disposition__c/recordTypes/Off_Market.recordType-meta.xml.
        NDA__c has exactly three picklists after this tranche. Party_Role__c defaults to Buyer
        HERE and only here: DPEG collects one NDA from the prospective buyer and one from the
        introducing broker, and counter-signs both.

        No <businessProcess> - that element is for Opportunity, Case, Lead and Solution only.

        POST-DEPLOY, NOT DEPLOYABLE: profile record-type assignment (System Administrator
        included - Modify All Data is an OBJECT permission and confers no record-type access),
        the per-profile default, the layout assignment, and the migration of existing NDA rows
        off Master. profiles/** is .forceignore'd in this repo.

        =====================================================================
        AMENDED 2026-08-24 - THREE VALUES ADDED, ONE OF THEM (Approved) A REPO CATCH-UP FOR AN
        ORG EDIT MADE OUTSIDE THIS REPO, TWO (Prepare, Review) NEW ON THE USER'S INSTRUCTION.
        =====================================================================

        Approved was already live on this record type in the org (confirmed via the UI API
        picklist-values endpoint - see objects/NDA__c/fields/Status__c.field-meta.xml's own
        2026-08-24 comment for why that endpoint and not a RecordType retrieve). This file did not
        carry it; it now does, matching the org.

        Prepare and Review are genuinely new and DISPOSITION_NDA ONLY - they are not added to
        Acquisition_NDA, which stays at its existing four values (Pending, Received, Signed,
        Sent). The user described these as the pre-send lifecycle: a Disposition NDA is now
        Prepared, then Reviewed, then Approved, and ONLY THEN does it enter the existing
        Not Sent -> Sent -> Signed sequence, with Declined remaining a terminal off-ramp reachable
        at any point after the document exists.

        ORDER BELOW IS THEREFORE Prepare -> Review -> Approved -> Not Sent -> Sent -> Signed ->
        Declined. THIS IS A DELIBERATE REORDER, NOT ONLY AN ADDITION: in the org as measured
        immediately before this change, Approved sat AFTER Not Sent (Not Sent, Approved, Sent,
        Signed, Declined). It now sits BEFORE Not Sent. Whether this list order actually controls
        the runtime Status__c dropdown order for this record type, independently of the master
        field's own value order, was not verified as part of this change - the same open question
        Acquisition_NDA's 2026-08-16 amendment flags for itself; see that file's comment.

        DEFAULT IS UNCHANGED - still Not Sent, not Prepare. Adding earlier lifecycle steps did not
        move where a newly-created Disposition NDA record starts; that is a separate decision left
        to the user, not inferred here.

        ROLLUP AND RELEASE-GATE IMPACT CHECKED, NO CHANGE NEEDED: flows/NDA_Signed_Rollup counts
        any Status__c value that is not literally 'Signed' or 'Declined' toward NDA_Count__c only
        (its default "Outstanding" branch), so Prepare/Review/Approved rows count as outstanding,
        not signed, and All_NDAs_Signed_Before_Progression keeps blocking every forward stage on
        this disposition until they reach Signed. See the field file's own 2026-08-24 comment for
        the full trace.
    -->
    <fullName>Disposition_NDA</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>The sell-side NDA, parented by Disposition. States (process order): Prepare, Review, Approved, Not Sent, Sent, Signed, plus Declined as a terminal off-ramp. Pending and Received are restricted out. See the XML comment in this file.</description>
